    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Dogs are not just pets—they can also help children learn to read. Research shows that reading with a dog can make a real difference."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Camille Rousseau, a doctoral student, studied 17 children in Grades 1 to 3. She asked them to read aloud with a dog and its "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "handler",
            "zh": "引导员"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " present, and again with only the handler. Before the study, each child was tested so they could read stories that were "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "slightly",
            "ref": "v-slightly"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " challenging."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The results were clear: children spent more time reading and showed greater "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "persistence",
            "ref": "v-persistence"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " when a dog was in the room. They also said they felt more interested and more "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "capable",
            "ref": "v-capable"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Why are dogs so helpful? It may be because they listen without judging. When children make mistakes, they feel less pressure around dogs. “If they’re practicing with a dog, they don’t mind making the mistake,” said Francine Alexander, a children’s book "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "publisher",
            "ref": "v-publisher"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "More schools and libraries are now using "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "therapy",
            "ref": "v-therapy"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " dogs in reading programs. Rousseau hopes her research will help children who "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "struggle",
            "ref": "v-struggle"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " with reading. Her study was the first to use carefully selected challenging books. She continues to study how dogs can support learning in other ways."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "i1",
        "type": "imageRef",
        "image": "img1"
      }
    ]
